# SOLID Principles — Senior Engineer / Architect Perspective

**“The hardest part of software engineering is not making a system work.
The hardest part is making sure the system can continue to work when everything around it starts changing.”**

Business requirements change.
Customers change their expectations.
Regulations change.
External systems change.
Teams change.
Technology changes.

And yet, our software is expected to continue evolving without becoming increasingly fragile.

This is where I see the real value of **SOLID principles**.

I don't see SOLID as five rules that we mechanically apply while writing Java classes.

I see SOLID as a **design philosophy for managing change, controlling coupling, and protecting the boundaries of a software system.**

Because in real production systems, the first version is rarely the biggest challenge.

The complexity emerges from the second, third and hundredth change.

A regulatory rule changes.

A new partner is onboarded.

A database is migrated.

An external API changes its contract.

A new business workflow is introduced.

And suddenly, a change that should have affected one component starts propagating across multiple modules.

So, at an experienced engineering level, I think the fundamental question behind SOLID is:

> **“How do I design my system so that change remains localized instead of becoming contagious?”**

That is the perspective from which I approach the five SOLID principles.

---

## S — Single Responsibility Principle

The Single Responsibility Principle is commonly expressed as:

> **“A class should have one reason to change.”**

I deliberately emphasize **“reason to change”**, rather than simply saying “one responsibility.”

Let me take an example from an insurance claims platform.

Imagine we have a `ClaimProcessingService`.

A claim may go through several stages:

* validating the claim,
* evaluating policy coverage,
* calculating the eligible settlement amount,
* performing fraud assessment,
* generating documents,
* notifying the customer.

A naïve implementation might put all of these responsibilities into one large service.

It may work perfectly well initially.

But now imagine that three independent things happen.

The regulatory team changes the claim eligibility rules.

The fraud-detection provider changes its API.

And the document-generation format changes.

These are three completely different sources of change.

If all of them require modifications to the same class, we have created unnecessary coupling.

The problem isn't simply that the class is large.

The deeper problem is that **multiple independent forces are changing the same unit of code.**

A better design could establish meaningful boundaries:

```text
ClaimValidator
       |
       v
CoverageEvaluator
       |
       v
SettlementCalculator
       |
       v
FraudAssessmentGateway
       |
       v
ClaimDocumentGenerator
```

Now each component has a much more cohesive reason to change.

If the fraud provider changes, I shouldn't have to modify settlement calculation.

If regulatory rules change, I shouldn't have to modify document generation.

This is where SRP becomes valuable from an architectural perspective.

It helps us identify **independent axes of change**.

And there is an important qualification.

SRP does not mean:

> “Every class should be tiny.”

Nor does it mean:

> “Every method deserves its own class.”

If we over-apply SRP, we can create excessive fragmentation, unnecessary indirection and a system that becomes harder to navigate.

So my interpretation of SRP is:

> **Group things that change together, and separate things that change independently.**

The goal is not smaller classes.

The goal is **high cohesion and controlled change**.

