# L — Liskov Substitution Principle

The Liskov Substitution Principle is often the most misunderstood SOLID principle.

At a high level, it says that a subtype should be usable wherever its base abstraction is expected without violating the behavioral expectations of the system.

Let's take an example from a banking system.

Imagine we have:

```java
interface Account {
    Money withdraw(Money amount);
}
```

And then we introduce:

```text
SavingsAccount
CurrentAccount
FixedDepositAccount
```

At first glance, inheritance may look perfectly reasonable.

But a fixed deposit account may not support arbitrary withdrawals in the same way as a savings account.

If the implementation simply throws an exception whenever `withdraw()` is called, we have a design problem.

The problem isn't really the implementation.

The problem is that **the abstraction promised behavior that every subtype cannot genuinely provide.**

This is where LSP becomes much deeper than inheritance.

It is about **behavioral contracts**.

An abstraction doesn't merely define method signatures.

It establishes expectations around:

```text
Valid inputs
Supported operations
Postconditions
Invariants
Observable behavior
```

If every caller has to start writing:

```java
if (account instanceof FixedDepositAccount) {
    ...
}
```

then our abstraction is probably wrong.

We might instead model capabilities more accurately:

```text
Account
   |
   +---- BalanceReadable
   |
   +---- Withdrawable
```

Now only accounts that genuinely support withdrawal implement `Withdrawable`.

This gives us a powerful architectural lesson:

> **A good abstraction should represent a valid behavioral contract, not merely a collection of common methods.**

So when I evaluate inheritance, I don't ask:

> “Can this class technically extend that class?”

I ask:

> **“Can this subtype genuinely honor the semantic contract of the abstraction?”**

That is the essence of LSP.

