# I — Interface Segregation Principle

The Interface Segregation Principle says:

> **Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not actually use.**

Consider an enterprise document-management platform.

Suppose we have a large interface:

```java
interface DocumentService {

    void upload();

    void download();

    void archive();

    void delete();

    void digitallySign();

    void classify();

    void performOCR();

    void generatePreview();
}
```

Now imagine an OCR processing component.

It only needs:

```text
performOCR()
```

But because of the large interface, the component is coupled to every other capability exposed by that contract.

This becomes particularly problematic in large organizations where multiple teams consume different parts of a shared interface.

Instead, we can model capabilities independently:

```text
DocumentReader
DocumentWriter
DocumentSigner
DocumentClassifier
OCRProcessor
PreviewGenerator
```

Now each client depends only on the capability it actually requires.

This reduces the **dependency surface area**.

And dependency surface area is an important form of coupling.

The more unnecessary knowledge one component has about another component, the more opportunities we create for changes to propagate.

But again, I wouldn't interpret ISP as:

> “Make every interface as small as possible.”

That can result in excessive fragmentation.

The better question is:

> **“Do these clients have different needs, different responsibilities, or different reasons to change?”**

If they do, separate contracts can provide meaningful architectural value.

If they don't, splitting the interface may simply introduce ceremony.

So I think of ISP as designing **client-driven contracts** rather than giant capability-oriented interfaces.

