# D — Dependency Inversion Principle

Finally, we come to the Dependency Inversion Principle.

From an architectural perspective, this is one of the most powerful SOLID principles.

The basic idea is:

> **High-level business policy should not depend directly on low-level implementation details. Both should depend on abstractions.**

Let's take an example from a financial-services risk engine.

The business policy might look like:

```text
Evaluate Customer
       |
       v
Apply Risk Rules
       |
       v
Determine Risk Category
       |
       v
Approve / Reject / Manual Review
```

Now imagine the risk engine directly depends on:

```text
Oracle Database
REST API
Redis
Third-party Credit Bureau SDK
```

Our business logic is now coupled to infrastructure.

A change in infrastructure can therefore force changes in business code.

Instead, the application layer can define what it needs:

```java
interface CreditProfileProvider {
    CreditProfile fetch(CustomerId customerId);
}
```

The business logic depends on that abstraction.

Infrastructure can provide different implementations:

```text
CreditBureauProvider
CachedCreditProfileProvider
MockCreditProfileProvider
AlternativeCreditProvider
```

Now the business policy is protected from infrastructure volatility.

And here I want to make an important distinction:

> **Dependency Injection is not Dependency Inversion.**

Dependency Injection is a technique.

For example:

```java
public RiskEngine(CreditProfileProvider provider) {
    this.provider = provider;
}
```

A framework such as Spring can provide that dependency.

But Dependency Inversion is a broader architectural principle.

The important questions are:

> **Who owns the abstraction?**

and

> **Which direction do the dependencies flow?**

A framework can perform dependency injection perfectly while the architecture is still badly coupled.

DIP is therefore fundamentally about **dependency direction and dependency ownership**, not simply using `@Autowired`.

