# Bringing the Five Principles Together

Now, when I step back and look at the five principles together, I don't see five unrelated rules.

I see a coherent approach to managing software complexity.

```text
                    SOLID
                      |
       ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
       |              |              |
    Cohesion        Coupling       Change
       |              |              |
       └──────────────┼──────────────┘
                      |
              Maintainability
                      |
                Evolvability
```

SRP asks:

> **What should change independently?**

OCP asks:

> **How can I isolate expected variation from stable behavior?**

LSP asks:

> **Does my abstraction actually preserve the behavioral contract?**

ISP asks:

> **What does each client really need to know?**

DIP asks:

> **Where should dependencies point, and who should own the abstraction?**

Together, these principles influence some of the most important characteristics of a production system:

**cohesion, coupling, extensibility, testability, maintainability and changeability.**

---

# SOLID Is Not a Checklist

And this is perhaps the most important lesson I would take away from SOLID after years of engineering experience.

I don't want a development team to look at a class and ask:

> “Do we have an interface?”

> “Do we have dependency injection?”

> “Do we have five separate classes?”

That is not architecture.

SOLID principles are **decision-making tools**, not compliance rules.

Sometimes introducing an abstraction is valuable.

Sometimes it is unnecessary.

Sometimes duplication is cheaper than premature abstraction.

Sometimes a single class can legitimately contain several closely related operations.

And sometimes the simplest design is genuinely the most maintainable design.

So I prefer to start with the system's business requirements and its expected change characteristics.

I ask:

**What is stable?**

**What is volatile?**

**What changes together?**

**What needs to evolve independently?**

**Where are my dependency boundaries?**

**Which abstractions actually protect the system from change?**

Only then do I decide which SOLID principle provides value.

---

# Closing Perspective

So, if I had to summarize SOLID in one statement, I would say:

> **SOLID is not about creating more classes, more interfaces, or more abstraction.**

> **It is about creating the right boundaries so that responsibilities remain cohesive, dependencies remain intentional, abstractions remain trustworthy, and changes remain localized.**

Because change is inevitable.

Requirements will change.

Technology will change.

Teams will change.

Business models will change.

External systems will change.

We cannot design a system that never changes.

But we can design a system where **change has a controlled blast radius**.

And that, ultimately, is how I think about SOLID.

**Good architecture does not eliminate change.
Good architecture makes change affordable.**

And when I apply SOLID in a real production system, my objective is not to make the code look more sophisticated.

My objective is to make the system **easier to understand today, safer to change tomorrow, and capable of evolving without continuously accumulating unnecessary complexity.**

That, for me, is the real essence of SOLID.

